    "url": "/platforms/salesforce/",
    "meta": {
      "title": "Salesforce Development Company | Clickmasters",
      "description": "Salesforce development — custom Apex & Lightning work, integrations that end the swivel-chair, org rescues & honest extend-vs-exit guidance from a firm that builds both."
    },
    "content": "---\n\n> **PAGE:** `/platforms/salesforce/`  \n> **SOURCE FILE:** `wave0-batch10/plat03-salesforce.md`\n\n\n# PLATFORM 03 — Salesforce\n\n\n\n# Salesforce Development Company\n\n**Salesforce development spans custom Apex and Lightning work inside the platform, the integrations that connect it to the rest of your company, and — the conversation most Salesforce shops won't have — the honest analysis of when to extend the org versus when the platform economics have turned against you.** Clickmasters does all three for US companies, and the third is our differentiator: because we also build [custom CRMs](/solutions/crm-development/), our extend-vs-exit advice carries no platform allegiance — we profit either way, so the math gets to be the math.\n\nThe state most Salesforce orgs reach, described without judgment: **years of admins, consultants, and acquisitions have produced an org nobody fully understands — hundreds of custom fields (half abandoned), automation layers stacked across generations (Workflow Rules under Process Builder under Flow), per-seat costs climbing annually, and the swivel-chair problem the platform was bought to solve, reborn between Salesforce and everything it doesn't talk to.** From that state, three paths exist, and each is sometimes right: *rationalize* (org cleanup and re-architecture — often the highest-ROI move nobody sells), *extend* (custom development and integration that makes the platform earn its fees), or *exit* (staged migration to [owned software](/compare/salesforce-vs-custom-crm/) when seat-count math has flipped). The [assessment](#assessment) prices all three against your renewal date. That's the page in one paragraph; details follow.\n\n[Trust bar: Salesforce projects delivered · certified engineers (if real) · orgs rescued]\n\n## Our Salesforce Services\n\n- **Custom development (Apex, Lightning Web Components)** — the logic and UX the platform's clicks can't reach: complex pricing, custom interfaces, [the quoting workflows](/solutions/crm-development/) that make sales actually live in the org\n- **[Integration development](/services/api-development/)** — the center of our Salesforce practice: ERP/accounting sync (NetSuite/QuickBooks/SAP), [customer-portal](/solutions/customer-portal/) connections, marketing and telephony, and the operational systems whose absence causes the swivel-chair — all with [the reconciliation discipline](/services/api-development/) (Salesforce and the ERP *will* agree, daily, in a report)\n- **Org rationalization & rescue** — the archaeology engagement: field-usage analysis, automation-layer consolidation onto Flow, technical-debt triage, documentation the org never had — typically recovering admin hours and unblocking every future project\n- **Portals & Experience Cloud alternatives** — customer/partner experiences on the platform where licensing makes sense, or [custom portals integrated to Salesforce](/solutions/customer-portal/) where Experience Cloud seat math doesn't — costed side by side, honestly\n- **[AI on your Salesforce data](/services/ai-application-development/)** — the pragmatic version: [lead-response agents](/solutions/ai-agents/) writing to the org with approval gates, call/email summarization into records, RAG over your Salesforce knowledge — via APIs, model-agnostic, [without waiting for platform-native pricing to make sense](/resources/ai-development/ai-cost-optimization/)\n- **Migration engineering — in or out** — consolidating acquired CRMs into Salesforce, or [the staged exit](/solutions/crm-development/) with history preserved to the record — [the same migration discipline](/services/legacy-software-modernization/), either direction\n\n## The Renewal-Date Conversation\n\nSalesforce decisions have a natural clock: the renewal. Our assessment is designed to land before yours with the three paths priced — rationalization scope, extension roadmap, or exit crossover math ([the full framework](/compare/salesforce-vs-custom-crm/)) — because the alternative is the default path: another year of drift, renewed at list price, decided by calendar instead of analysis. Even clients who stay (most do) enter the renewal with leverage and a plan; that alone tends to pay for the assessment.\n\n## Recent Salesforce Work\n\n> **[PLACEHOLDER — 2–3 case studies: integration scope with reconciliation outcomes, org rescue with admin-hours recovered, an honest exit story with seat-fee math — verifiable]**\n\n## Engagement & Pricing\n\nOrg assessment: **$5K–$12K** fixed (usage analysis, debt map, three-paths pricing) · integration projects: **$15K–$80K** · custom development: scoped per feature · rationalization programs: **$25K–$90K** · [exit programs priced on the CRM page](/solutions/crm-development/). [Salesforce engineer staffing](/services/it-staff-augmentation/) available for teams with their own roadmap.\n\n## FAQ\n\n**Are you a Salesforce partner or a Salesforce critic?**\nNeither and both — we're a software firm. Salesforce is often the right answer, especially rationalized and integrated; sometimes the seat math has flipped and owned software wins. Our only allegiance is to the assessment's arithmetic, which is precisely what makes it useful.\n\n**Our org is a mess but works. Why touch it?**\nBecause \"works\" is expensive: every new project quotes high against the debt, admin hours leak into archaeology, and the automation stack is one acquisition away from unmaintainable. Rationalization is boring, high-ROI, and makes everything after it cheaper — it's the engagement we recommend most and vendors sell least.\n\n**Can you connect Salesforce to our ERP so people stop re-keying?**\nThat's our flagship Salesforce engagement: bidirectional sync with field-mapping sign-off, [idempotent operations](/resources/software-engineering/idempotency-in-apis/), and daily reconciliation reports. The swivel-chair retires; finance and sales read the same numbers.\n\n**When does leaving Salesforce actually make sense?**\nThe profile: seat fees in five-plus figures annually, a permanent consultant retainer fighting the platform, core workflow living in spreadsheets *beside* the org, and customization needs that are really [software needs](/solutions/crm-development/). Below that profile, stay and rationalize — we'll say so plainly. [The crossover math →](/compare/salesforce-vs-custom-crm/)\n\n**Can AI work with our Salesforce today?**\nYes, via API — [agents with approval gates](/solutions/ai-agents/), summarization into records, measured accuracy — model-agnostic and priced per task, without betting your AI roadmap on any single vendor's bundle. [The engineering →](/services/ai-agent-development/)\n\n---\n\n### [#assessment] Book a Salesforce Assessment\nBring the org, the seat count, and the renewal date — leave with the three paths priced and the leverage that comes with them.\n[Form] [Calendar embed]",
